<section xml:id="HW3" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Homework 3</title>

  <p><alert> Due Thursday, February 12</alert></p>

<exercises xml:id="hw3-ex">
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
        [From the book]: Chapter 11: 6.5, 8.2
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
        <statement>
      <p>
    [Problem A]: Let <m>  R</m>   be an integral domain. If there exist rings <m>  A</m>   and <m>  B</m>   such that <m>  R=A\times B</m>  , prove that <m>  A=0</m>   or <m>  B=0</m>  . 
      </p>
</statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
        <statement>
      <p>
    [Problem B]: Let <m>  A</m>   and <m>  B</m>   be two rings. 
<ol>
<li>Let <m>  I</m>   be an ideal of <m>  A</m>   and <m>  J</m>   an ideal of <m>  B</m>  . Prove that <m>  I\times J</m>   is an ideal of <m>  A\times B</m>   and <me>  \frac{A\times B}{I\times J}\cong \frac{A}{I}\times \frac{B}{J}</me>  . 
</li>
<li>Let <m>  L</m>   be an ideal of <m>  A\times B</m>  . Prove there exists an ideal <m>  I</m>   of <m>  A</m>   and an ideal <m>  J</m>   of <m>  B</m>   such that <m>  L=I\times J</m>  .
</li>
<li> <p> With <m> L=I\times J</m>   as above, prove that <m>  L</m>   is a maximal ideal ideal of <m>  A\times B</m>   if and only if one of the following holds:
</p> <ol>
<li> <m>  I=A</m>   and <m>  J</m>   is a maximal ideal of <m>  B</m>  ; or
</li>
<li><m>  J=B</m>   and <m>  I</m>   is a maximal ideal of <m>  A</m>  .
</li>
</ol>
</li>
</ol>
      </p>
</statement>
<hint>
    <p>
        For part (c), you may find Problem A useful.
    </p>
</hint>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p> 
       [Problem C]: (This is a modified version of problem 6.8 in Chapter 11) Let <m>  I</m>   and <m>  J</m>   be ideals of a ring <m>  R</m>   such that <m>  I+J=R</m>  . 
<ol>
<li>Prove <m>  IJ=I\cap J</m>   (The product ideal <m>  IJ</m>   was defined in Exercise 3.13)
</li>
    <li> Prove the <em>
    Chinese Remainder Theorem</em>: For any pair <m>  a, b</m>   of elements of <m>  R</m>   there is an element <m>  x\in R</m>   such that <m>  x-a\in I</m>   and <m>  x-b\in J</m>  . 
 </li>
<li> Prove that <m>  R/IJ</m>   is isomorphic to <m>  R/I\times R/J</m>  . 
</li>
<li> State and prove (by induction) a generalization of the statement in part (c) to <m>  n</m>   ideals <m>  I_1, \dots, I_n</m>   such that <m>  I_i+I_j=R</m>   for all <m>  i\ne j</m>  . 
</li>
</ol>     
        </p>
     </statement>
     </exercise>

<exercise>
        <statement>
      <p>
    [Problem D]: Prove that <me>  \frac{\mathbb R[x]}{(x^4-1)}\cong \mathbb C\times \mathbb R\times \mathbb R</me>  .
 </p>
</statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
      <p>
    [Problem E]: Let <m>R</m> be a ring and let <m>\mathcal{Q}(R)</m> denote its ring of fractions, as defined in class. We proved in class multiplication is well-defined. Prove that addition is well-defined as well. 
</p>
</statement>
    </exercise>


</exercises>
</section>